<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shunzhi Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1638">1638</a>–<a href="/years/1661">1661</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aisin-gioro">House of Aisin Gioro</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shunzhi Emperor was the first emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Dynasty</a> to rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, enthroned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1644 while still a child. During his reign, regents and generals continued campaigns against Ming loyalists in the south. Accounts of the Qing Empire place his reign at the start of Qing rule in China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shunzhi-emperor.jpg" alt="Shunzhi Emperor" width="256" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B8%85%E4%B8%96%E7%A5%96%E9%A1%BA%E6%B2%BB%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Shunzhi Emperor, personal name Fulin, was the ninth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> and the grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a> system. When Hong Taiji died on 21 September 1643 without naming a successor, a committee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> princes chose the five-year-old Fulin and appointed two co-regents, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorgon">Dorgon</a> and Jirgalang. His mother was the Khorchin Mongol Bumbutai, honoured as Empress Dowager Zhaosheng. Dorgon took control of the government and, by early June 1644, firm control of the military.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In June 1644 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorgon">Dorgon</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, ordered his Banner troops not to loot and had all claimants to the Ming throne executed. The Shunzhi Emperor was enthroned in Beijing in November 1644. Under Dorgon the Qing subdued almost all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a> loyalists into the far south-west, despite unpopular measures such as the hair-cutting edict of 1645. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>, who had taken Beijing, was killed in September 1645.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorgon">Dorgon</a> died on 31 December 1650, and the emperor then ruled personally. He announced a purge of corruption in April 1651, issued the Six Edicts in 1652, encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> to take part in government and in 1658 re-established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanlin-academy">Hanlin Academy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-secretariat">Grand Secretariat</a>. Court factions frustrated his efforts. Ming resistance revived in the 1650s, but by 1661 his armies had defeated the last enemies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a> and the Prince of Gui, both of whom died the following year. In the late 1650s he became a fervent follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Chan Buddhism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His favourite consort, Donggo, died in September 1660. The emperor contracted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> on 2 February 1661, chose his seven-year-old third son Xuanye as heir and died on 5 February at the age of twenty-two. His will named four regents, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oboi">Oboi</a>, Soni, Suksaha and Ebilun, but they and the empress dowager clearly tampered with it before it was published. It expressed regret for his Chinese-style rule and neglect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> traditions. Rumours that he had retired to a monastery circulated, but the evidence suggests that his death was not staged.</p><p>Citing the will, the regents abolished the Thirteen Eunuch Bureaus, eliminated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanlin-academy">Hanlin Academy</a> and limited the Deliberative Council to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. Because fewer documents survive from his reign than from later ones, it is a relatively little-known period of Qing history. He was succeeded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shunzhi Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the regent Dorgon, the Qing moved quickly: they captured Beijing, enthroned the young <b>Shunzhi Emperor</b> as ruler of China and began decades of campaigns against Ming loyalist resistance in the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qing proclaimed the <b>Shunzhi Emperor</b> there that October, renamed principal buildings to stress harmony rather than supremacy, made the nameplates bilingual in Chinese and Manchu and brought shamanist worship into the Palace of Earthly Tranquility; under the Qing the Hall of Supreme Harmony served only for ceremonies…</blockquote><a href="/work/forbidden-city">Forbidden City · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Empress Dowager Xiaozhuang, mother of the <b>Shunzhi Emperor</b>, made most decisions for her twelve-year-old son and proved a skilled political operator.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mukden Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Hall of Mental Cultivation, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.8,123.45&amp;year=1652" aria-label="Mukden Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7554 675 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shunzhi Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8004 945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8004 945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.8,123.45&amp;year=1652">What was here in 1652? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
